{"created": "2025-07-28T20:13:14.936400+00:00", "modified": "2026-04-22T21:08:12.405987+00:00", "id": 16050919, "conceptrecid": "16050918", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.16050919", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.16050918", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16050919", "metadata": {"title": "COMPARISON OF BIOMECHANICAL PARAMETERS AND IN-SPORT ANALYTICS IN BASEBALL HITTERS", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.16050919", "publication_date": "2025-07-17", "description": "<p>This study aimed to investigate the relationships between ground reaction force (GRF) moments and baseball hitting performance metrics, including bat speed and exit velocity, in NCAA Division I athletes. Sixteen players completed maximal-effort swings off a pitching machine while standing on dual force plates. Markerless motion capture synchronized with GRF data was used to calculate peak propulsive and braking GRF moments in the frontal and transverse planes relative to each batter&rsquo;s center of mass. These moments were then correlated with average bat speed and exit velocity at two pitch heights: high and low in the strike zone. The frontal plane braking moment showed a significant (<em>p&nbsp;</em>&lt; 0.05) moderate correlation with exit velocity for low-zone pitches. The transverse plane propulsive moment was significantly correlated with bat speed for both pitch heights, and the frontal plane propulsive moment was significantly correlated with bat speed for high-zone pitches. Among all four moments, only frontal plane braking demonstrated a significant association with exit velocity. These findings suggest that GRF moments contribute differently to swing outcomes depending on pitch location, with braking moments supporting energy transfer through the kinetic chain and propulsive moments driving rotational movement. To the best of current knowledge, this is the first study to examine braking moments in baseball hitting, providing new insight into how biomechanical factors relate to baseball hitting performance, as measured by bat speed and exit velocity.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Murdock, Alanna", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0008-8114-3818"}], "contributors": [{"name": "Lynn, Scott", "affiliation": null, "type": "Supervisor"}, {"name": "Hoffmann, Matt", "affiliation": null, "type": "Supervisor"}, {"name": "Noffal, Guillermo", "affiliation": null, "type": "Supervisor"}], "keywords": ["baseball hitting", "biomechanics", "ground reaction force moment", "bat speed", "exit velocity"], "language": "eng", "resource_type": {"title": "Thesis", "type": "publication", "subtype": "dissertation"}, "thesis": {"university": "California State University, Fullerton"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "communities": [{"id": "csuf"}], "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "16050918"}}]}}, "title": "COMPARISON OF BIOMECHANICAL PARAMETERS AND IN-SPORT ANALYTICS IN BASEBALL HITTERS", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/16050919", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/16050919", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/16050919?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16050919", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16050919", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.16050919", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/16050919/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/16050918", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/16050918", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16050918", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.16050918", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:16050919/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:16050919/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/16050919/files", "media_files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/16050919/media-files", "thumbnails": {"10": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:16050919:55%202025-07